tools.description:
Create a chart in one call: installs its reusable component, writes a named React cell, reloads and builds the default app. Supports cartesian, donut, scatter, barline, heatmap, and waterfall charts. Start new charts with the closest supported configuration, then use file tools to customize the generated cell or component when needed. When adding props to a built-in data-display component or a fork, also add them to its sibling Name.interface.json when users are likely to tweak them and they make sense as clearly labeled controls. Keep callbacks and implementation-only props in code. Edit existing cells in place. Identical catalog files are reused; differing owned files stop creation with a diff before cell, SQL, or parameter files are written. Preserve local customizations when resolving conflicts. Supply a short descriptive name for the notebook cell. Supply data as {type: "artifact", name} for an existing checked table artifact or {type: "sql", query, source} for SQL. Aggregate, filter and rank upstream before charting. Field mappings must match column names exactly. Returns cellName for a named-cell screenshot and source paths for customization. Does not mount the chart in an app layout or publish. On failure re-list/read attempted paths before retrying; repair existing files instead of creating duplicates. A successful build establishes compilation, not data or visual correctness. For static HTML hosting, read back the uploaded source and compare it with the intended content, then inspect the rendered result; do not invent SQL or DAG work. For data-backed apps, also evaluate relevant artifacts to check calculations. Use get_code_project_screenshot for the preview and get_code_project_console to investigate runtime errors. Inspect the rendered image for correct content, readable labels, missing data, clipping, and overlaps; obtaining a screenshot alone is not verification. Initial-render capture does not test interactions; test changed controls when browser interaction is available. Reuse successful checks while relevant source and data remain unchanged; repeat affected checks after changes or unresolved failures. Report only checks actually performed.

